From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josep Maria Blasco <josep.maria.blasco@epbcn.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 19:22:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] WebAssembly port: JavaScript objects as Rexx objects (.js,
 .JSObject)

js.cls lets Rexx programs use JavaScript objects with ordinary messages,
in the browser build and in the Node command-line build:

    say .js~Math~max(3, 7)                       -- 7
    m = .js~Map~new; m~set("a", 1); say m~size   -- 1
    resp = .js~fetch("data.json")~await          -- a Promise, waited for
    fs = .js~require("node:fs")                  -- Node: require, npm
    doc = .js~page~document                      -- browser: the page's DOM
    p = doc~createElement("p"); p~textContent = "Hola"; doc~body~appendChild(p)
    a~sort(.js~handler(obj, "COMPARE"))          -- a Rexx comparator (synchronous)
    btn~addEventListener("click", .js~handler(obj, "CLICKED"))   -- page: queued
    .js~eventLoop                                -- events, until .js~stopEventLoop
    ::requires "js.cls"

.js is globalThis of the realm (the Web Worker that hosts the run in the
browser, Node's main thread under Node); in the browser .js~page is the
page's window, with the DOM.  Objects of different realms do not mix.
Objects, functions, arrays and Promises are .JSObjects, by reference; strings, numbers, booleans (1/0) and
bigints come back as Rexx strings, null as .nil, undefined as
.js~undefined (a single object; a missing property reads as it).  Rexx
strings always go as strings (.js~num, .js~bool, .js~bigint, .js~bytes,
.js~undefined force a type, and .js~box(type, x) with the type indicators
of BSF4ooRexx's box(): BOolean, BYte, Char, Double, Float, Int, Long,
Object, SHort, STring, Number, BIgint, shortened down to their capitals;
.js~unbox gives the Rexx value back); Arrays and Directories/StringTables are
copied as arrays and plain objects.

- Message names arrive uppercased, so they are resolved case-insensitively
  along the prototype chain (cached per object; of two matches the one
  starting in lowercase wins, window.document over window.Document; else an
  error).  o~name gets a property and calls it if it is a function (not a
  constructor: .js~Map~new); o~name(args) calls; o~name = v sets an
  existing property.  Exact access: o["name"], o["name"] = v,
  o~invoke("name", args...).  o[i] and o[i] = v on an array or typed
  array count from 1, as a Rexx Array and BSF4ooRexx's Java arrays
  (o[1] is JavaScript's o[0]); a list (NodeList...) keeps 0.  ~at(i)
  and ~put(v, i) are the same as o[i] and o[i] = v, as in Rexx's
  collections.  Also ~new, ~await, ~typeOf, ~className,
  ~instanceOf, ~hasProperty, ~propertyNames, ~makeArray (DO OVER: an
  iterable's values), ~supplier (DO WITH INDEX i ITEM v OVER: a Map's keys
  and values, an array's 1..n (as o[i]), a list's 0..n-1, another
  iterable's 1..n, an object's properties), ~byteString; names that do not hide common JavaScript ones (Map's
  get/set/has/keys, Response's bytes).
- A JavaScript exception, or a rejected Promise under ~await, is SYNTAX
  98.900 "JavaScript error: ..." with what was thrown in ADDITIONAL[2].
- wasm/web-js.cpp: the package "js"; .JSObject's methods are native (errors
  point at the caller's line).  Each operation goes synchronously to the
  main runtime thread (emscripten_proxy_sync_with_ctx), which may finish it
  later (~await); several Rexx threads may use it at once.
- wasm/js-realm.js: a realm, the same code in the worker, Node and the
  page: a handle table (one id per object, so == is identity; counted,
  released when the .JSObject is garbage collected, through UNINIT), name
  resolution, value conversion.  Promises handed to Rexx are marked handled
  (Node ends the process on an unhandled rejection).
- wasm/js-bridge.js (--pre-js of both builds, after js-realm.js): the
  dispatcher on the main runtime thread.  Requests for the page realm go to
  the page by postMessage (ooRexx-worker.js), ooRexx.js answers from its
  own realm (js-realm.js, loaded on first use), and the interpreter's call
  is finished then; the worker never blocks.
- Browser: each realm off unless the page grants it, ooRexx.run({js:
  {worker: true, page: true}}); a granted program has all of JavaScript's
  power there (in the worker fetch with the site's cookies, IndexedDB,
  eval; in the page the DOM).  Node: always on; require() resolves from the
  current directory.
- Handlers: .js~handler(obj, msg [, options]) is a .JSHandler that becomes,
  in whatever realm it is passed to, a function (one per handler and realm:
  removeEventListener works) that sends msg to obj with the call's
  arguments.  In the worker's realm and under Node it is synchronous, as
  .net~handler: called while a Rexx thread waits for JavaScript (sort,
  forEach, map, dispatchEvent) the method runs on that thread and
  JavaScript gets its result (a condition is thrown as a RexxError), and
  that thread cannot wait for JavaScript meanwhile (~await, the page:
  errors); called by JavaScript on its own (a timer, a Node server) it runs
  on a server thread attached to the interpreter for the call, one at a
  time, and JavaScript does not wait (it gets undefined): the realm's
  thread must keep its event loop for what finishes later (sockets, the
  RexxAPI daemon, the page).  Requests reach the main runtime thread
  through the bridge's own queue, taken in a microtask (Emscripten's
  proxying queue is not reentrant), so that while it waits for a handler it
  can take both.  With option queued (implied by latest and max), and
  always in the page, whose thread cannot wait, the function queues the
  call (js_event; from the page by postMessage) and returns undefined at
  once.  .js~nextEvent([seconds]) takes the next one (a .JSEvent; ~dispatch
  sends msg to obj with the call's arguments), .js~eventLoop dispatches
  until .js~stopEventLoop; each event goes to one waiting thread.  Options
  preventDefault, stopPropagation, stopImmediatePropagation act during the
  browser's dispatch; option latest keeps at most one event of the handler
  waiting, the newest, and max n at most n, dropping the oldest.  A
  RexxError nobody catches (in an event listener) is written on the
  standard error.  A handler is kept from its first trip to JavaScript
  until ~release (its waiting events are dropped) or the end of the run; in
  the browser its functions do nothing after the run.  Under Node the
  program's end ends the process (Emscripten only sets exitCode, so a
  listening server kept Node alive).  .JSObject's methods are unguarded (a
  thread waiting in .js~eventLoop must not hold .js).
- wasm/extra-packages.cpp: orx_wasm_extra_packages() now lists the
  packages a build links (jdor, js) through weak symbols; web-jdor.cpp
  provides orx_wasm_jdor_package().
- Tests: wasm/tests/web-api.py (11 cases, 5 of them the page realm),
  qualify-node-cli.sh (require of an npm module, import, await, errors; an
  HTTP server answered from Rexx, a Rexx comparator, events taken by
  several threads;
  iteration, options latest and max, release).  Documented in wasm/README.md.
