+## JavaScript objects: `.js`, `.JSObject` (both builds)
+
+`js.cls` lets Rexx use JavaScript objects with ordinary messages, in the
+browser build and in the Node command-line build:
+
+```rexx
+say .js~Math~max(3, 7)                       -- 7
+m = .js~Map~new; m~set("a", 1); say m~size   -- 1
+resp = .js~fetch("data.json")~await          -- a Promise, waited for
+fs = .js~require("node:fs")                  -- Node: require, npm modules
+::requires "js.cls"
+```
+
+* `.js` is `globalThis` of the realm: the Web Worker that hosts the run in
+  the browser (fetch, IndexedDB, WebSocket, timers; no DOM), Node's main
+  thread under Node (`.js~require(name)` from the current directory,
+  `.js~import(name)`).  In the browser `.js~page` is the page's `window`,
+  with the DOM (`doc = .js~page~document; p = doc~createElement("p")`);
+  objects of the two realms do not mix (an argument must belong to the
+  realm of the object that gets the message).  Every object, function, array or Promise is a
+  `.JSObject`, by reference; strings, numbers, booleans (1/0) and bigints
+  come back as Rexx strings, null as .nil, undefined as `.js~undefined`
+  (a single object; a property that does not exist reads as it, and
+  `~hasProperty` tells the two apart).
+* ooRexx uppercases message names, so they are resolved case-insensitively
+  along the prototype chain (cached per object; of two matches the one
+  starting in lowercase wins, `window.document` over `window.Document`,
+  else it is an error).  `o~name` gets a property, calling it (with this = o) if it is a
+  function, except constructors (Capitalized, with a prototype:
+  `.js~Map~new`); `o~name(args)` calls a method; `o~name = v` sets an
+  existing property.  Exact: `o["name"]`, `o["name"] = v`,
+  `o~invoke("name", args...)` (`o~invoke(.nil, ...)` calls o itself).
+  `o[i]` and `o[i] = v` on an array or typed array count **from 1**, as a
+  Rexx Array and BSF4ooRexx's Java arrays (`o[1]` is JavaScript's `o[0]`;
+  0 is an error); a list (NodeList, HTMLCollection) keeps JavaScript's
+  `o[0]`, and JavaScript's methods keep their own (`o~slice(1)`).  Also
+  `o~new(...)`, `o~await`, `o~typeOf`, `o~className`, `o~instanceOf(c)`,
+  `o~hasProperty(n)`, `o~propertyNames`, `o~makeArray` (`DO x OVER o`: an
+  iterable's values, as `for...of`, so a Map's `[key, value]` pairs; an error
+  for anything else), `o~supplier` (`DO WITH INDEX i ITEM v OVER o`: a Map's
+  keys and values, an array's 1..n and items (as `o[i]`), a list's 0..n-1
+  (as its `o[i]`), another iterable's 1..n and values, other objects' own
+  properties), `o~byteString`; names chosen not to hide common JavaScript ones (a Map's
+  get/set/has/keys).  Rexx's own Object methods (send, start, copy, ...)
+  are not passed on: `o~invoke("send", x)`.
+* Rexx strings always go as strings; `.js~num(x)`, `.js~bool(x)`,
+  `.js~true`, `.js~false`, `.js~bigint(x)`, `.js~bytes(x)` (a Uint8Array),
+  `.js~undefined` force a type; so does `.js~box(type, x)` with the type
+  indicators of BSF4ooRexx's `box()` (`BOolean`, `BYte`, `Char`, `Double`,
+  `Float`, `Int`, `Long`, `Object`, `SHort`, `STring`, and JavaScript's
+  `Number` and `BIgint`; caseless, shortened down to their capitals: `"bo"`
+  is `"Boolean"`; whole numbers checked against Java's ranges, `Long` within
+  JavaScript's safe integers), and `.js~unbox(o)` gives the Rexx value back
+  (also of a JavaScript `Number`, `String`, `Boolean` or `BigInt` object).
+  An Array goes as an array, a Directory or StringTable as a plain object
+  (copies), .nil as null.
+* A JavaScript exception (or a rejected Promise under `~await`) is SYNTAX
+  98.900 "JavaScript error: TypeError: ..."; what was thrown is
+  `condition("O")~additional[2]`.
+* Several Rexx threads may use it at once; each `~await` blocks only its
+  own thread.
+* **Browser: off unless the page grants it**, realm by realm:
+  `ooRexx.run({..., js: {worker: true, page: true}})`.  A granted program
+  can do everything JavaScript can there: in the worker, fetch with the
+  site's cookies and its IndexedDB; in the page, the DOM and whatever the
+  page's own scripts can.  Grant it to code you trust as you trust your own
+  scripts (the playground does: users run their own code).  Under Node it is
+  always on.
+* Handlers: `.js~handler(obj, "MSG" [, options])` is a function for
+  JavaScript (one per handler and realm, so `removeEventListener` works)
+  that sends `MSG` to `obj` with the call's arguments.  In the worker's realm
+  and under Node it is synchronous, as `.net~handler`: called while a Rexx
+  thread waits for JavaScript (`a~sort(.js~handler(self, "COMPARE"))`,
+  `forEach`, `map`, `dispatchEvent`), the method runs on that thread and
+  JavaScript gets its result (a condition is thrown as a `RexxError`);
+  meanwhile that thread cannot wait for JavaScript (`~await`, the page:
+  errors).  Called by JavaScript on its own (a timer, a Node server, a
+  Promise settling), the method runs at once on a thread of its own (one
+  call at a time, attached to the interpreter for the call), may wait for
+  JavaScript, and JavaScript gets undefined: the realm's thread cannot wait
+  for it, since what finishes later (sockets, the RexxAPI daemon under Node,
+  the page) needs that thread's event loop.  A condition JavaScript does not
+  pass on (there, or in an event listener) is written on the standard error.
+* Queued handlers: with option `queued`, and always in the page (whose
+  thread may not block), the function queues the call and returns undefined
+  at once, and a Rexx thread takes the calls: `.js~eventLoop` (sends each
+  until `.js~stopEventLoop`) or `ev = .js~nextEvent([seconds])` +
+  `ev~dispatch`.  The browser has finished the dispatch by the time Rexx
+  sees it (`event~currentTarget` is .nil, `event~target` is fine), so what
+  must happen during it is an option: `.js~handler(obj, msg,
+  "preventDefault stopPropagation stopImmediatePropagation")` (for
+  synchronous handlers too); option `latest` keeps at most one event of the
+  handler waiting, the newest (mousemove, scroll, resize), and `max n` at
+  most n, dropping the oldest (`max 0` is an error; given both, `latest`
+  wins); both imply `queued`.  Each event goes to one waiting thread; an
+  error in a handler ends `eventLoop` as any error.  A handler stays alive
+  from its first trip to JavaScript until `handler~release` (its events
+  still waiting are dropped) or the end of the run; in the browser its
+  functions do nothing once the run is over.  Under Node the program's end
+  is the process's end, even with a server listening or a timer pending.
+* Rexx objects in JavaScript: any Rexx object that is not converted (a
+  string or number, an Array, a Directory, a StringTable) goes by reference,
+  as a `RexxProxy` (`js-realm.js`): `o.name(args)` sends `NAME`, and
+  BSF4ooRexx's `sendMessage(name, [args])`, `sendMessage0`..`5`,
+  `sendMessageScope(name, scope, [args])`; `String(o)` is its string when it
+  came over.  Calls run as a synchronous handler's; in the page, which
+  cannot wait, a call is queued as an event (`C` "call id" + the call) and
+  gives a Promise: the Rexx thread taking events runs it (`JsNextEvent`), and
+  so does a Rexx thread waiting for JavaScript, for the calls that come
+  meanwhile; the answer goes back to the page (`Module.jsCallReply`) and
+  settles the Promise (a condition: a `RexxError`; at the end of the run,
+  rejected).  One proxy per Rexx object while JavaScript holds it;
+  the object is pinned in `web-js.cpp`, counted each time it goes, and
+  JavaScript gives the counts back when it collects the proxy
+  (`FinalizationRegistry`, an event `Z`).  Tests: `tests/js-proxy.rex` (28),
+  and the release under Node (`--expose-gc`) in `qualify-node-cli.sh`.
+* Rexx classes extending JavaScript classes: `.js~extend(.MyClass, base
+  [, names...])`, once per class (its `ACTIVATE` class method is the natural
+  place), with `MyClass` a subclass of `JSObject` and `base` a JavaScript
+  class (a `.JSObject`) or its name as a script sees it (`"Car"`, also for a
+  `class Car {}` at a script's top level).  As `.net~extend` in ooRexx/.NET:
+  it makes a JavaScript class extending `base`, named as the Rexx class, and
+  answers it.  Every method, getter (`NAME`) and setter (`"NAME="`) of
+  `base`'s prototype chain whose name matches, case-insensitively, a public
+  method of `MyClass` (or of its superclasses below `JSObject`; not `INIT`,
+  `UNINIT`, `UNKNOWN`) is overridden: JavaScript calls the Rexx method, as a
+  synchronous handler (on the waiting Rexx thread, or on a thread of its own
+  without waiting).  `names` are exact JavaScript names the base does not
+  have, added as methods (`connectedCallback`).  `.MyClass~new(args...)`
+  makes the JavaScript object with `new` and `args` (`MyClass`'s `INIT`
+  calls `self~init:super(args...)`); a base constructor that calls an
+  override reaches the Rexx method, and `self` can already be used there.
+  `self~name:super(args)` is the base's member (the methods of a mixin
+  class, private, which the class inherits).  The instance comes back from
+  JavaScript as the Rexx object itself.  The two keep each other alive until
+  `.js~detach(o)`; after it, and for instances JavaScript makes itself, the
+  base's members run.  Only where JavaScript can wait for Rexx: the
+  worker's realm and Node, not the page.  Tests: `tests/js-extend.rex` (31).
+* Rexx from the page (browser): `rexx.start(opts)` (`ooRexx.js`, the options
+  of `run()`) is a Promise of a session, a run of `.js~session` (`js.cls`),
+  which hands the page a `.JSSession` (operation 17, `SESSION`) and takes
+  events for good.  The session has what `run()` returns and `eval(source,
+  ...args)` (a routine: `USE ARG`, `RETURN`), `routine(name, source)`,
+  `loadPackage(name [, source])`, each a Promise; what they make sees
+  `js.cls` and the public classes and routines made before (a parent
+  package they are added to).  `<script type="text/rexx">` (inline or
+  `src`): `ooRexx.js` starts a session for the page's scripts (both realms
+  granted, output to the console) and runs them in order, each as a
+  routine; `OoRexx.scripts` is a Promise of it.  Tests: `tests/web-api.py`
+  (session eval, session page, rexx scripts with `tests/web-scripts.html`).
+* Implementation: `web-js.cpp` is the package `js` (`.JSObject`'s methods
+  are native, so errors point at the caller's line); each operation is
+  passed synchronously to the main runtime thread, where `js-bridge.js` (a
+  `--pre-js`) keeps a handle table per realm (one id per object, so `==` is
+  identity; counted, released when the `.JSObject` is garbage collected),
+  resolves names, converts values and may finish later (`~await`).  The
+  realm itself is `js-realm.js`, the same code in the worker, Node and the
+  page; `js-bridge.js` dispatches: requests for the page realm go to the page
+  by `postMessage` (`ooRexx.js` loads `js-realm.js` there on first use and
+  answers), and the interpreter's call is finished when the answer comes
+  back (about 0.3 ms a round trip in Chromium).  Requests reach the main
+  runtime thread through a queue of the bridge, taken in a microtask, not
+  through Emscripten's proxying queue, which is not reentrant: while that
+  thread waits for a synchronous handler it keeps taking both (the pthreads'
+  system calls go through Emscripten's).  A synchronous handler's call costs
+  about 0.1 ms; a queued handler's function hands its event to `js_event`
+  (from the page by `postMessage`), which queues it for `.js~nextEvent`.
+
 ## Known limitations / deliberate non-claims
 
 * WebSocket transport (browser, and the MEMFS build under Node): listening
@@ -296,6 +464,8 @@ BUILD_DIR/bin-node-test/rexx testOORexx.rex -R ooRexx/API
 * UDP/datagram semantics are qualified only in the Node command-line build.
 * Browser: ADDRESS commands requiring an OS child process fail closed (RC 127);
   ADDRESS JDOR draws (see above).
+* `.JSObject` is in the browser build and the Node command-line build, not
+  in the MEMFS runtime of the CMake build (`bin/`).
 * Maximum recursion depth is set by the JS engine's native stack (internal
   CALL / internal function calls / method calls): Node about 7,100 / 5,400 /
   4,000; browser workers about 900 / 700 / 500 in Chromium, 1,300 / 900 / 700
