+/*                                                                            */
+/*----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+/*
+ * ooRexx/WASM: the dispatcher of the JavaScript bridge (.JSObject, see
+ * wasm/web-js.cpp and wasm/web/js.cls), a pre-js of the browser build and of
+ * the Node command-line build, after wasm/js-realm.js.  It runs on the main
+ * runtime thread, which never blocks, and passes each request of the
+ * interpreter to its realm:
+ *
+ *  - the local one: "worker" in the browser (the Web Worker that hosts the
+ *    run), "node" under Node;
+ *  - "page" (browser): the page's main thread, which has the DOM.  The
+ *    request goes there by postMessage (Module.orxJsPage, set by
+ *    ooRexx-worker.js), ooRexx.js answers with Module.jsPageReply(id, bytes),
+ *    and only then the interpreter's call is finished;
+ *  - granted: in the browser the page must allow each realm
+ *    (ooRexx.run(..., {js: {worker: true, page: true}})): it gives the Rexx
+ *    program all of JavaScript there.  Under Node "node" always is.
+ *
+ * Which realm: GLOBAL names it; most ops start with their target object,
+ * whose reference carries its realm; REQUIRE, IMPORT and VALUE are local.
+ * Released handles (X records) go to their realm.
+ *
+ * Events: the queued handlers' functions of the local realm, and those of the
+ * page (forwarded by ooRexx-worker.js as Module.jsEvent(bytes)), hand their
+ * event to the interpreter's queue (js_event in web-js.cpp).  So do the
+ * page's calls of Rexx (Module.jsCallQueued(id, bytes), a RexxProxy's method,
+ * ooRexx.js's session): the Rexx thread taking events runs them, and their
+ * answer comes back to Module.jsCallReply, which passes it to the page.  A synchronous
+ * handler of the local realm runs its Rexx method through js_sync_call:
+ * called while a request of a Rexx thread is being done (serving), on that
+ * thread, and this thread waits for the result (the only place where
+ * JavaScript waits for Rexx), doing the requests that come meanwhile; called
+ * on its own, on the server thread, without waiting.  A Rexx thread that
+ * JavaScript waits for cannot wait for JavaScript: its requests that would
+ * finish later (a Promise, the page) are errors.
+ */
