+/*
+ * JavaScript objects as Rexx objects, in ooRexx/WASM (browser and Node):
+ *
+ *    ::requires "js.cls"
+ *    say .js~Math~max(3, 7)                      -- 7
+ *    m = .js~Map~new; m~set("a", 1); say m~size  -- 1
+ *    say .js~Object~keys(.js~newObject(d))~join  -- d: a Directory
+ *    resp = .js~fetch("data.json")~await         -- a Promise, waited for
+ *    fs = .js~require("node:fs")                 -- Node
+ *
+ * .js is the realm's globalThis (the Web Worker that runs ooRexx in the
+ * browser, Node's main thread under Node); .js~page is the page's window in
+ * the browser (the DOM):
+ *
+ *    doc = .js~page~document
+ *    p = doc~createElement("p"); p~textContent = "Hola"; doc~body~appendChild(p)
+ *
+ * In the browser the page that runs ooRexx must grant each realm (run option
+ * js: {worker: true, page: true}): it gives the program everything
+ * JavaScript can do there.  Objects of different realms do not mix (an
+ * argument must belong to the realm of the object that gets the message).
+ *
+ * Every JavaScript object, function, array or Promise is a .JSObject (by
+ * reference, never copied).  Messages to it:
+ *   o~name          property name; if it is a function, called with this = o
+ *                   (but a constructor, Capitalized with a prototype, is
+ *                   returned as is: .js~Map~new)
+ *   o~name(a, b)    method name called with a, b
+ *   o~name = v      property name set (it must exist; else o["name"] = v)
+ Names are case-insensitive (ooRexx uppercases them); exact, with no guessing:
+ *   o["name"]       property name; o[i] for an array's (or typed array's)
+ *                   element i, from 1 as in a Rexx Array (o[1] is JavaScript's
+ *                   o[0]); a list (NodeList...) keeps JavaScript's o[0]
+ *   o["name"] = v   property name set (created if need be)
+ *   o~at(i), o~put(v, i)   the same as o[i] and o[i] = v, as in Rexx's
+ *                   collections (JavaScript's own Array at(): o~invoke("at", i))
+ *   o~invoke("name", args...)   method name called; o~invoke(.nil, args...)
+ *                   calls o itself (a function)
+ * and o~new(args...) (new o(...)), o~await (a Promise's value; a rejection is
+ * an error), o~typeOf, o~className, o~instanceOf(c), o~hasProperty("name"),
+ * o~propertyNames (Object.keys), o~makeArray (DO x OVER o: the values of an
+ * iterable, as JavaScript's for...of, so a Map's [key, value] pairs; an error
+ * for anything else), o~supplier (DO WITH INDEX i ITEM v OVER o: a Map's keys
+ * and values, an array's 1..n and items (as o[i]), a list's 0..n-1 (as its
+ * o[i]), any other iterable's 1..n and values, any other object's own
+ * properties), o~byteString (an ArrayBuffer's or typed array's bytes as a
+ * Rexx string).  These names were
+ * chosen not to hide common JavaScript ones (Map's get/set/has/keys, ...).
+ * Methods of Rexx's Object (send, start, copy, run, request, ...) and at/put
+ * are not passed on: use o~invoke("send", x).
+ *
+ * Values: JavaScript strings, numbers, booleans (1/0) and bigints come back as
+ * Rexx strings, null as .nil and undefined as .js~undefined (one object, so
+ * x == .js~undefined; a property that does not exist gives it, and
+ * o~hasProperty("name") tells the two apart).  Rexx strings go as strings,
+ * always (also "42"); .js~num(x), .js~bool(x), .js~true, .js~false,
+ * .js~bigint(x), .js~bytes(x) (a Uint8Array), .js~undefined force a type, and
+ * so does .js~box(type, x) with BSF4ooRexx's type indicators (BOolean, BYte,
+ * Char, Double, Float, Int, Long, Object, SHort, STring; also Number, BIgint;
+ * caseless, shortened down to the capitals; whole numbers checked against
+ * Java's ranges); .js~unbox(o) gives a box's Rexx value back; an
+ * Array goes as an array, a Directory or StringTable as a plain object (copies),
+ * .nil as null; .js~newArray(items...) and .js~newObject(directory) make them
+ * in JavaScript (.js~Array and .js~Object are JavaScript's own).  Any other
+ * Rexx object goes by reference, as a RexxProxy (below).  A JavaScript exception is SYNTAX 98.900 "JavaScript error:
+ * TypeError: ..."; condition("O")~additional[2] is what was thrown.
+ *
+ * Rexx objects in JavaScript: a RexxProxy, with BSF4ooRexx's names:
+ * o.sendMessage(name, [args]), o.sendMessage0(name) ... sendMessage5(name,
+ * a1, ..., a5), o.sendMessageScope(name, scope, [args]) (scope a Rexx
+ * class); and any other property is a function that sends that message,
+ * upper-cased (o.deposit(50)).  String(o) is the object's string when it last
+ * came over (o.string() asks now).  A call runs as a synchronous handler
+ * (a condition is a RexxError; called by JavaScript on its own, it gives
+ * undefined).  In the page, which cannot wait, a call is queued as an event
+ * and gives a Promise: the Rexx thread taking events (.js~eventLoop,
+ * .js~nextEvent) runs it, and the Promise gets its result (rejected with a
+ * RexxError for a condition; while nobody takes events, it waits; when the
+ * run ends, it is rejected).  The same Rexx object
+ * is the same proxy, and a proxy back in Rexx is the object itself; the
+ * object lives while JavaScript holds its proxy.
+ *
+ * Handlers: .js~handler(object, message [, options]) is a .JSHandler; given
+ * to JavaScript, it is a function there (the same one each time, so
+ * removeEventListener works) that sends the message to the object with the
+ * call's arguments.  In the worker's realm and under Node it is synchronous,
+ * as .net~handler: the method runs at once.  Called while a Rexx thread waits
+ * for JavaScript (a comparator, forEach, map, dispatchEvent), it runs on that
+ * thread, and JavaScript gets its result (a condition is thrown as a
+ * RexxError); meanwhile it cannot wait for JavaScript in turn (~await, the
+ * page).  Called by JavaScript on its own (a timer, a Node server, a Promise
+ * settling), it runs on a thread of its own, one call at a time, and may wait
+ * for JavaScript; JavaScript does not wait for it (it gets undefined), and a
+ * condition is written on the standard error:
+ *
+ *    a~sort(.js~handler(self, "COMPARE"))      -- COMPARE answers each comparison
+ *
+ * With option "queued" (implied by latest and max), and always in the
+ * page (whose thread cannot wait), the function queues the call and returns
+ * undefined at once, and a Rexx thread takes the calls:
+ *
+ *    btn~addEventListener("click", .js~handler(self, "CLICKED"))   -- the page
+ *    .js~eventLoop                     -- until .js~stopEventLoop
+ *    ::method clicked
+ *      use arg event                   -- the arguments of the JavaScript call
+ *
+ * A queued handler cannot answer anything (no sort comparators), and by the
+ * time Rexx sees an event the browser has dispatched it (event~currentTarget
+ * is .nil; event~target is fine): what must happen during the dispatch goes
+ * in the options, words "preventDefault", "stopPropagation" and
+ * "stopImmediatePropagation" (for synchronous handlers too).  Option "latest" keeps at most one event of
+ * the handler waiting, the newest (for mousemove, scroll, resize, ...), and
+ * "max n" at most n, the newest n (older ones are dropped).  .js~nextEvent([seconds]) waits for the next
+ * event (none given: as long as it takes) and returns a .JSEvent (or .nil
+ * when the time is over); event~dispatch sends the handler's message to its
+ * object with the call's arguments.  .js~eventLoop does that over and over,
+ * on the thread that called it, until .js~stopEventLoop (from a handler or
+ * another thread); an error in a handler ends it, as any error.  Several
+ * threads may wait: each event goes to one.  A handler is kept alive from
+ * the first time it goes to JavaScript until handler~release (its calls still
+ * waiting, and later ones, are dropped) or the end of the run; in the browser the functions do
+ * nothing once the run is over.
+ *
+ * Classes: a Rexx class can extend a JavaScript class, as with .net~extend
+ * in ooRexx/.NET (only where JavaScript can wait for Rexx: the worker's
+ * realm, Node):
+ *
+ *    ::class MyCar subclass JSObject
+ *    ::method activate class
+ *      .js~extend(self, "Car")             -- a JavaScript class, or its name
+ *    ::method init
+ *      use arg name, year
+ *      self~init:super(name, year)         -- new Car(name, year)
+ *    ::method age                          -- JavaScript's calls of age() come here
+ *      return self~age:super + 1           -- Car's age()
+ *
+ * .js~extend(class, base [, names...]) makes a JavaScript class extending
+ * base, named as the Rexx class, and answers it.  Every method, getter
+ * (NAME) and setter ("NAME=") of base's prototype chain whose name matches,
+ * case-insensitively, a public method of the class (or of its superclasses
+ * below JSObject; not INIT, UNINIT, UNKNOWN) is overridden: JavaScript calls
+ * the Rexx method as a synchronous handler.  names: exact JavaScript names
+ * the base does not have, added as methods.  .MyCar~new(args...) makes the
+ * JavaScript object (a base constructor that calls an override reaches the
+ * Rexx method, with self usable); self~name:super is the base's member (a
+ * mixin's private methods); the instance comes back from JavaScript as the
+ * Rexx object itself.  Both live until .js~detach(o); after it, and for
+ * instances JavaScript makes itself, the base's members run.
+ *
+ * Sessions (the browser): ooRexx.js's start() runs .js~session, which hands
+ * the page a .JSSession (a RexxProxy there) and takes events for good, so
+ * the page's calls run one at a time, in order:
+ *
+ *    s~eval(source, args...)        -- source as a routine (USE ARG, RETURN): its result
+ *    s~routine(name, source)        -- a Routine (r.call(args...) in JavaScript)
+ *    s~loadPackage(name [, source]) -- a Package (its prolog run): from source, or a file
+ *
+ * What these make sees js.cls and the public classes and routines of what
+ * the session made before (later code sees earlier definitions, as the
+ * scripts of a page share their globals).
+ */
+call JsSetup .JSObject, .JSTyped, .JSHandler, .JSEvent, .JSTyped~undefined
+.environment~js = .JSGlobal~new(JsDefaultRealm(), 0, "global")
+
+::requires "js" LIBRARY
+
+/** A JavaScript object (or function, array, Promise, symbol) of a realm. */
+::class JSObject public
+::method extensions class           -- class -> its JavaScript class (.js~extend)
+  expose extensions
+  if \var("EXTENSIONS") then extensions = .IdentityTable~new
+  return extensions
+::method extensionOf class          -- the JavaScript class of a class or of a superclass, or .nil
+  use strict arg cls
+  do while cls \== .JSObject, cls~isSubclassOf(.JSObject)
+    ext = self~extensions[cls]
+    if ext \== .nil then return ext
+    cls = cls~superClass
+  end
+  return .nil
+::method init
+  expose jsref jsrealm jsdisplay
+  if self~class \== .JSObject then do
+    ext = .JSObject~extensionOf(self~class)
+    if ext \== .nil then do        -- .MyClass~new(args...): new MyClass(...args) in JavaScript
+      signal on syntax
+      self~jsBind(JsConstruct(self, ext, arg(1, "A")))
+      return
+    end
+  end
+  use strict arg jsrealm, id, jsdisplay
+  jsref = jsrealm || "09"x || id
+  return
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method jsBind unguarded           -- for the bridge: the JavaScript object of an extending class's instance
+  expose jsref jsrealm jsdisplay
+  use strict arg ref
+  if var("JSREF") then return
+  parse var ref realm "09"x id "09"x display
+  jsdisplay = display
+  jsrealm = realm
+  jsref = realm || "09"x || id
+::method jsRef                      -- realm<tab>id, for the bridge
+  expose jsref
+  return jsref
+::method realm                      -- worker, node (page)
+  expose jsrealm
+  return jsrealm
+::method string                     -- a JSObject (HTMLDivElement #12, worker)
+  expose jsdisplay jsrealm
+  return "a JSObject ("jsdisplay"," jsrealm")"
+::method "=="                       -- the same JavaScript object
+  expose jsref
+  use strict arg other
+  if \other~isA(.JSObject) then return .false
+  return other~jsRef == jsref
+::method "\=="
+  use strict arg other
+  return \(self == other)
+::method "="
+  forward message "=="
+::method "\="
+  forward message "\=="
+::method "<>"
+  forward message "\=="
+::method "><"
+  forward message "\=="
+::method hashCode
+  expose jsref
+  return jsref~hashCode
+::method unknown unguarded          external "LIBRARY js js_unknown"
+::method "[]" unguarded             external "LIBRARY js js_get"
+::method "[]=" unguarded            external "LIBRARY js js_put"
+::method at unguarded               external "LIBRARY js js_get"   -- AT is [] in Rexx
+::method put unguarded              external "LIBRARY js js_put"   -- PUT is []=
+::method invoke unguarded           external "LIBRARY js js_call"
+::method new unguarded              external "LIBRARY js js_new"
+::method await unguarded            external "LIBRARY js js_await"
+::method typeOf unguarded           external "LIBRARY js js_typeof"
+::method className unguarded        external "LIBRARY js js_classname"
+::method propertyNames unguarded    external "LIBRARY js js_keys"
+::method makeArray unguarded        external "LIBRARY js js_makearray"
+::method supplierData private unguarded external "LIBRARY js js_supplier"
+::method supplier unguarded         -- DO WITH INDEX i ITEM v OVER o
+  signal on syntax
+  data = self~supplierData
+  return .supplier~new(data[2], data[1])
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method byteString unguarded       external "LIBRARY js js_bytes"
+::method instanceOf unguarded       external "LIBRARY js js_instanceof"
+::method hasProperty unguarded      external "LIBRARY js js_has"
+::method uninit unguarded           external "LIBRARY js js_uninit"
+
+/** .js: the default realm's globalThis, and the bridge's helpers.  Their
+    errors are raised in the caller (RAISE PROPAGATE), not in this file. */
+::class JSGlobal public subclass JSObject
+::method worker unguarded           -- the realm of the Web Worker running ooRexx (browser)
+  signal on syntax
+  return JsGlobal("worker")
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method node unguarded             -- Node's realm
+  signal on syntax
+  return JsGlobal("node")
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method page unguarded             -- the page's window (browser; the page must grant it)
+  signal on syntax
+  return JsGlobal("page")
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method require unguarded          -- Node's require(name), from the current directory
+  signal on syntax
+  use strict arg name
+  return JsRequire(name)
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method import unguarded           -- import(name): a Promise of the module
+  signal on syntax
+  use strict arg name
+  return JsImport(name)
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method new unguarded              -- .js~new(constructor, args...)
+  signal on syntax
+  use arg ctor
+  if \ctor~isA(.JSObject) then raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~new: the constructor must be a JSObject")
+  return ctor~sendWith("NEW", arg(2, "A"))
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method num unguarded              -- force a JavaScript number
+  use strict arg x
+  return .JSTyped~new("F", x)
+::method bool unguarded             -- force a boolean (1/0, true/false)
+  use strict arg x
+  return .JSTyped~new("B", x)
+::method true unguarded
+  return .JSTyped~new("B", 1)
+::method false unguarded
+  return .JSTyped~new("B", 0)
+::method bigint unguarded
+  use strict arg x
+  return .JSTyped~new("I", x)
+::method bytes unguarded            -- a Uint8Array with these bytes
+  use strict arg x
+  return .JSTyped~new("Y", x)
+::method undefined unguarded       -- JavaScript's undefined: always the same object
+  return .JSTyped~undefined
+::method str unguarded              -- x as a string
+  use strict arg x
+  return x~string
+::method box unguarded              -- .js~box(type, x): x as a JavaScript value of that type
+  -- the type indicators of BSF4ooRexx's box(), caseless and shortened down
+  -- to their capitals (BOolean, BYte, Char, Double, Float, Int, Long, Object,
+  -- SHort, STring), and JavaScript's own Number and BIgint
+  signal on syntax
+  use strict arg type, x
+  t = type~strip~upper
+  names = .array~of("BOolean", "BYte", "Char", "Double", "Float", "Int", "Long", -
+                    "Object", "SHort", "STring", "Number", "BIgint")
+  name = ""
+  do n over names
+    if abbrev(n~upper, t, n~verify("ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ") - 1) then do
+      name = n; leave
+    end
+  end
+  if name == "" then raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~box: unknown type" type"; one of" -
+    names~makeString("L", ", "))
+  whole = .array~of("BYte", -128, 127, "SHort", -32768, 32767, -
+                    "Int", -2147483648, 2147483647, "Long", -9007199254740991, 9007199254740991)
+  select
+    when name == "Object" then do
+      if x == .nil | x~isA(.JSObject) | x == .JSTyped~undefined then return x
+      raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~box: an Object must be a JSObject, .nil or .js~undefined")
+    end
+    when name == "BOolean" then return self~bool(x)
+    when name == "STring" then return x~string
+    when name == "Char" then do
+      -- one character: one byte, or one UTF-8 sequence
+      if x~string~length > 0 & x~string~substr(2)~verify(xrange("80"x, "BF"x)) == 0 -
+         & (x~string~length == 1 | x~string~left(1) >= "C0"x) then return x~string
+      raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~box:" x "cannot be a Char")
+    end
+    when name == "BIgint" then do
+      if datatype(x, "W") then return self~bigint(x)
+      numeric digits 40
+      if datatype(x, "W") then return self~bigint(x)
+      raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~box:" x "cannot be a BigInt")
+    end
+    when name == "Number" | name == "Double" | name == "Float" then do
+      if datatype(x, "N") | wordpos(x~string~strip~upper, "NAN INFINITY +INFINITY -INFINITY") > 0 then
+        return self~num(x)
+      raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~box:" x "cannot be a" name)
+    end
+    otherwise do                      -- BYte, SHort, Int, Long: a whole number in range
+      i = whole~index(name)
+      numeric digits 20
+      if datatype(x, "W") then if x >= whole[i + 1] & x <= whole[i + 2] then return self~num(x)
+      raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~box:" x "cannot be a" name -
+        "(a whole number from" whole[i + 1] "to" whole[i + 2]")")
+    end
+  end
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method unbox unguarded            -- .js~unbox(o): the Rexx value of a box or of a JavaScript wrapper
+  use strict arg o
+  if o~isA(.JSTyped) then do
+    if o~kind == "B" then return wordpos(o~value~string~strip~lower, "1 true") > 0
+    return o~string
+  end
+  if o~isA(.JSObject) then do
+    c = o~className
+    if wordpos(c, "Number String Boolean BigInt") > 0 then return o~invoke("valueOf")
+  end
+  return o
+::method newArray unguarded         -- a new JavaScript array of the arguments
+  signal on syntax
+  return JsValue(arg(1, "A"))
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method newObject unguarded        -- a new plain JavaScript object from a Directory/StringTable
+  signal on syntax
+  use strict arg d = (.directory~new)
+  return JsValue(d)
+syntax: raise propagate
+::method extend unguarded           -- .js~extend(class, base [, names...]): see Classes above
+  signal on syntax
+  use strict arg cls, base, ...
+  if \cls~isA(.Class) then raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~extend: the first argument must be a class, found" cls)
+  if \cls~isSubclassOf(.JSObject) | cls == .JSGlobal then
+    raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~extend:" cls~id "must be a subclass of JSObject")
+  if .JSObject~extensions~hasIndex(cls) then
+    raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~extend:" cls~id "already extends" .JSObject~extensions[cls])
+  if \base~isA(.JSObject) & \base~isA(.String) then
+    raise syntax 98.900 array(".js~extend: the base must be a JSObject (a class) or the name of one, found" base)
+  -- the methods of the class and of its superclasses below JSObject, public
+  names = .Set~new
+  c = cls
+  do while c \== .JSObject
+    sup = c~methods(.nil)
+    do while sup~available
+      if sup~item \== .nil, \sup~item~isPrivate then names~put(sup~index)
+      sup~next
+    end
+    c = c~superClass
+  end
+  names~removeItem("INIT"); names~removeItem("UNINIT"); names~removeItem("UNKNOWN")
+  exact = .array~new
+  do i = 3 to arg()
+    exact~append(arg(i)~string)
+  end
+  r = JsExtend(base, cls~id, names~makeArray, exact)
+  ext = r[1]
+  -- self~name:super, the base's member: a mixin's private methods, after
+  -- JSObject in the search order (so found before its UNKNOWN)
+  mixin = .Object~mixinClass(cls~id "BASE")
+  extref = "'" || ext~jsRef~changeStr("09"x, "'||'09'x||'") || "'"
+  do o over r[2]
+    parse var o kind "09"x jsname "09"x rexxname "09"x hasBase
+    jsname = jsname~changeStr("'", "''")
+    if hasBase \== 1 then iterate
